\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What the supplied-circle limit establishes}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{The [fixed-mode limit](fdip-004J) compares finite operators and
one Gaussian moment with a declared circle. Its length, measure,
mesh spacing, factor #{n^2} and positive mass are supplied.
Without #{n^2}, each fixed-mode Laplacian eigenvalue tends to zero.
This explains why a finite positive operator alone does not determine
a continuum scale or geometry.}

\p{The quantifier on the frequency matters. For even #{n} and
#{k=n/2}, the discrete eigenvalue is #{4n^2}, whereas the continuum
frequency has eigenvalue #{4\pi^2k^2=\pi^2n^2}. Their ratio stays
#{4/\pi^2}; the fixed-mode proof is therefore not a relative
uniform estimate over all modes. It proves neither operator-norm
resolvent convergence nor convergence of determinants or Gaussian
measures. Removing the positive mass or adding interactions requires
additional arguments.}

\p{For comparison, Weisz distinguishes the critical-point and
phase-diagram control needed for low-energy lattice-QCD continuum
limits from the renormalization questions for composite observables
and high-energy scales
\citet{Section 1.1, printed p. 2}{weisz2010renormalization}.
The circle calculation is a much smaller prescribed approximation:
it contains no interacting critical-point analysis. It establishes no
emergent geometry, reflection positivity, Lorentzian reconstruction,
Standard Model dynamics or gravitational dynamics. Those identifications
remain among the [open physical interfaces](fdip-003Y).}
